introduction:
  bigQuestion: "Can you solve a problem that doesn't exist yet?"
  overview: "This unit prepares you for <strong>Paper 1, Section B (HL Extension)</strong>. Unlike SL, you must dive deeper into the technical challenges (e.g., vSLAM, Neural Processing) and provide <strong>Strategic Justifications</strong> for your solutions. It's not just about knowing the technology; it's about evaluating its feasibility in a complex, real-world scenario."
  journey: "You'll start by researching the <strong>HL Extension Topics</strong> (specific to the current Case Study). Then you'll synthesize this research with your Core CS knowledge to build comprehensive, justified strategies for the client."

concepts:
  - title: "Deep Research (HL)"
    icon: "fa-book-open"
    content: |
      <p>Surface level isn't enough. You need expert evidence.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Academic Literacy:</strong> Reading white papers and journals.</li>
          <li><strong>Evidence Bank:</strong> Building a library of real-world deployments (e.g., "How NASA uses vSLAM").</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Strategic Justification"
    icon: "fa-scale-balanced"
    content: |
      <p>Why is your solution the BEST one? (Evaluation).</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Trade-offs:</strong> Acknowledgement that every choice has a cost (Latency vs Accuracy).</li>
          <li><strong>Operational Constraints:</strong> Does it work in the rain? Does the battery last?</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Technical Synthesis"
    icon: "fa-link"
    content: |
      <p>Connecting the dots.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Cross-Topic Logic:</strong> Linking Case Study challenges to Coursework concepts (e.g., "This Rescue Robot needs A* Pathfinding (Topic 5) and Sensors (Topic 7)").</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "The 12-Mark Essay"
    icon: "fa-pen-nib"
    content: |
      <p>Structuring a high-level argument.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Balanced Account:</strong> Arguing FOR and AGAINST your own solution.</li>
          <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> A final, decisive judgment backed by evidence.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Synthesis"
    definition: "The combination of ideas to form a theory or system (Linking Case Study to Syllabus)."
  - term: "Strategic Justification"
    definition: "Providing valid reasons or evidence to support a chosen course of action."
  - term: "Operational Constraints"
    definition: "Limitations imposed by the real-world environment (e.g., Battery life, Bandwidth, Weight)."
  - term: "Peer-reviewed Research"
    definition: "Articles written by experts and reviewed by other experts in the field (Gold standard evidence)."
  - term: "Scalability"
    definition: "The capability of a system to handle a growing amount of work."
  - term: "Feasibility"
    definition: "The state or degree of being easily or conveniently done."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "More Jargon is Better"
    correction: "Don't just list buzzwords. Explain HOW the technology solves the SPECIFIC problem in the scenario."
  - mistake: "Ignore the Drawbacks"
    correction: "A Level 7 answer ALWAYS admits the flaws. Perfect solutions don't exist in the real world."
  - mistake: "It's just an English Essay"
    correction: "No. It's a Technical Report disguised as an essay. You need specific details (Algorithms, Protocols, Specs)."

